    "body": "After securing $2.1 billion this May, AI-driven drug designer Isomorphic Labs is back with more money—a lot more—securing the industry’s largest private fundraise ever.\nIsomorphic Labs has clinched a whopping $5 billion in series C funding, more than doubling its own $2.1 billion series B from May and breaking the industry’s record for most money raised in a single round.\nThe $5 billion series C was co-led by California investors Greenoaks and Sequoia Capital, with continued investment from parent company Alphabet, according to a Tuesday release . Altos Labs, another AI-focused drug discovery engine, previously held the title for biggest fundraise after its $3 billion debut in 2022.\nFor Isomorphic, the fresh $5 billion will fuel efforts to scale the biotech’s drug design engine, which combines “a suite of foundational simulation AI models that natively work across multiple therapeutic areas and drug modalities,” according to the company.\nThe cash will also support Isomorphic’s preclinical pipeline as the biotech gears up to enter the clinic. Isomorphic has yet to publicly disclose any assets but said it has produced preclinical results for internal programs in both oncology and immunology.\nVenture capital\nAI-fueled Isomorphic bags $2.1B, the second largest biotech round ever\nIsomorphic Labs, which hasn’t yet disclosed a molecule or reached the clinic, breaks the recent trend of investors putting their money behind more mature and de-risked assets.\nMay 13, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nTristan Manalac\nRead more\nIsomorphic is also focused on building out an ecosystem of partners and global infrastructure “required to power the future of AI-led bioengineering.” So far, the Alphabet company has announced research partnerships with several Big Pharmas, including Novartis, Eli Lilly and Johnson & Johnson .\n“Our drug design engine is generating molecules with unprecedented precision and speed,” Demis Hassabis, Isomorphic’s founder and CEO, said in a prepared statement. The 2024 Nobel Laureate winner is also the co-founder of Google DeepMind and chief scientist for Alphabet.\n“This round enables us to accelerate our progress: building foundational biology models, deepening our systemic disease understanding, and driving our first therapeutic programs towards clinical trials to deliver tangible impact for patients,” Hassabis said.\nThe biotech intends to “unlock some of the hardest problems in drug discovery,” according to Isomorphic President Max Jaderberg.\nLondon-based Isomorphic launched in 2021 and shared news of its first fundraise of $600 million in March 2025. To date, the company has raised at least $7.7 billion—all without ever disclosing a clinical program.",
